    <body>
      <sec id="article-27940.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PHP) encompasses a heterogeneous group of rare inactivating parathormone (PTH)/parathormone-related peptide signaling disorders characterized by end-organ resistance to parathyroid hormone due to genetic or epigenetic alterations of the <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> locus. This course reviews the clinical presentation of PHP and related disorders, including hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH levels, often accompanied by features of Albright hereditary osteodystrophy, ectopic ossification, brachydactyly, and multisystem endocrine dysfunction, as well as their pathophysiology, which reflects impaired Gs&#x003b1;-mediated cAMP signaling, with variable imprinting-dependent expression and phenotypic heterogeneity across PHP subtypes and related conditions. Participants will gain an in-depth understanding of the interpretation of biochemical and genetic findings, modern principles for classifying parathormone-related peptide signaling disorders, and&#x000a0;complications, including neuromuscular irritability, skeletal abnormalities, and neurocognitive and metabolic sequelae.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance learners' competence in identifying PHP and related disorders, performing evidence-based evaluation and longitudinal monitoring, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach to managing this condition, with the aim of optimizing calcium-phosphate balance, growth, and endocrine outcomes.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical features of pseudohypoparathyroidism and related disorders.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret laboratory findings to distinguish pseudohypoparathyroidism from other causes of hypocalcemia.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement a management approach for pseudohypoparathyroidism&#x000a0;to prevent long-term morbidity.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate interprofessional management approaches to optimize&#x000a0;outcomes&#x000a0;in patients with&#x000a0;pseudohypoparathyroidism and related disorders.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27940&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27940">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PHP) is a rare inherited disorder characterized by target organ resistance or unresponsiveness to parathyroid hormone (PTH). Historically, PHP is the first hormone resistance syndrome described by Albright et&#x000a0;al The syndrome mimics hypoparathyroidism, with patients experiencing hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia; however, unlike hypoparathyroidism, serum PTH levels are elevated. PHP is part of a broader and heterogeneous group of disorders collectively known as inactivating PTH/PTHrP signaling disorders (iPPSD). This group includes related conditions, eg, pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism (PPHP), progressive osseous heteroplasia (POH), acrodysostosis, and osteoma cutis. These disorders are typically caused by de novo autosomal-dominant genetic mutations or by epigenetic, sporadic, or inherited alterations affecting the <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> gene or its regulatory regions, ie, key components of the PTH/PTHrP signaling pathway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>PHP&#x000a0;and related disorders are caused by genetic and epigenetic alterations that affect components of the PTH/PTHrP signaling pathway, most commonly involving the <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> locus.</p>
        <p>The former classification was based on the presence or absence of specific clinical and biochemical features, as well as on the in vivo response to exogenous PTH and the results of in vitro assays measuring Gs&#x003b1; protein activity. Based on this framework, these disorders were classified into PHP type 1, PHP type 2, and PPHP. PHP type 1 (PHP1) was further subdivided into PHP1A (Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man [OMIM]&#x000a0;#103580), PHP1B (OMIM #603233), and PHP1C (OMIM #612462). The presence or absence of Albright hereditary osteodystrophy (AHO) differentiated PHP1A and PHP1C from PHP1B, while the presence or absence of hormonal resistance differentiated PHP from PPHP. The in vivo response to exogenous PTH, assessed by nephrogenic cAMP generation and phosphaturia, distinguished PHP1 from PHP2, and in vitro assays measuring Gs&#x003b1; activity in erythrocyte membranes differentiated PHP1A from PHP1C.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>The previous classification of PHP did not account for molecular defects and omitted related conditions, eg, acrodysostosis, POH, and PTH1R-related chondrodysplasia. To address these limitations, the EuroPHP network established a new classification system encompassing all disorders caused by inactivation of the PTH/PTHrP signaling pathway, termed inactivating iPPSD.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;This nomenclature employs numerical identifiers to define specific subtypes, enabling characterization of both clinical and molecular features. The classification is based on underlying disease mechanisms and incorporates major and minor diagnostic criteria to aid in diagnosis (see <bold>Tables 1</bold>&#x000a0;and <bold>Table 2</bold>).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Genetic and Epigenetic Basis</bold>
</p>
        <p>This group of disorders is inherited in an autosomal dominant manner. The major subtypes of PHP and related disorders result from inactivating pathogenic variants in genes involved in the PTH/PTHrP signaling pathway or from epigenetic alterations at the <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> locus, which encodes the &#x003b1;-subunit of the stimulatory G protein (Gs&#x003b1;). The clinical phenotype is determined by the parental origin of the affected allele, reflecting the effects of genomic imprinting.</p>
        <p>PHP Ia, Ib, and Ic result from reduced or absent expression or function of Gs&#x003b1; encoded by the maternal <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> allele, due to one of the following mechanisms:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Inactivating <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> pathogenic variants, most commonly frameshift or missense mutations (causing PHP Ia), and mutations&#x000a0;affecting exon 13 (causing PHP Ic)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Defects&#x000a0;in imprinting control regions within the GNAS locus or the adjacent <italic toggle="yes">STX16</italic> gene (causing PHP Ib)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Paternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 20q (causing PHP Ib).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r5">[5]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>PPHP is associated with inactivating pathogenic variants affecting the paternal <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> allele, resulting in the absence of Gs&#x003b1; expression without hormone resistance. POH and osteoma cutis are also most commonly associated with paternal GNAS pathogenic variants.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27940.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
