      <sec id="article-27940.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>PHP and related disorders are relatively rare disorders. The global prevalence of PHP is uncertain. The estimated prevalence ranges from 0.62 to 1.47 cases per 100,000 population, depending on geographic location.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pathophysiology of PHP and related disorders is characterized by impaired signal transduction along the PTH/PTHrP&#x02013;Gs&#x003b1;&#x02013;cAMP pathway, resulting in end-organ resistance to parathyroid hormone and, in some cases, to other hormones that signal through the same pathway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Gs&#x003b1;, the predominant transcript of the <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> gene, primarily participates in the PTH/PTHrP&#x02013;cAMP&#x02013;PKA signalling pathway. In most tissues, Gs&#x003b1; is transcribed from both the paternal and maternal alleles. However, in specific tissues, eg, the renal proximal tubules, thyroid, ovaries, and pituitary, preferential expression of the maternal allele occurs.&#x000a0;Upon binding of PTH or PTHrP to the PTH1 receptor (PTH1R), activation of Gs&#x003b1; stimulates intracellular cyclic AMP (cAMP) generation, thereby initiating downstream signaling. This pathway plays a critical role in skeletal development, bone turnover, and mineral ion homeostasis.</p>
        <p>In the kidney, PTH binding to PTH1R stimulates the production of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3, inhibits phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule, and increases calcium reabsorption in the distal nephron. In the growth plate, PTHrP promotes endochondral ossification&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Haploinsufficiency of Gs&#x003b1; due to genetic or epigenetic alterations in the <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> gene underlies the pathophysiology of several PHP subtypes, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>In PHP Ia and PHP Ic, loss of expression of the maternal <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> allele results in impaired Gs&#x003b1;-mediated signaling, leading to resistance to PTH and other hormones that signal through the cAMP pathway.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In PHP Ib, imprinting defects cause tissue-specific reduction of Gs&#x003b1; expression, explaining isolated renal PTH resistance in the absence of AHO.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In PHP Ic, heterozygous <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> pathogenic variants do not impair intrinsic Gs&#x003b1; activity on in vitro assays; instead, they disrupt coupling of Gs&#x003b1; to its receptors, resulting in defective signal transduction despite normal enzymatic function.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In PPHP, loss of expression of the paternal <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> allele leads to skeletal features without hormone resistance. This pattern is consistent with preferential maternal allele expression of Gs&#x003b1; in the renal proximal tubules, thyroid, ovaries, and pituitary.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Heterotopic ossification is the most devastating feature of AHO. Gs&#x003b1; plays a critical role in ectopic ossification, as reduced Gs&#x003b1; levels in human mesenchymal stem cells increase osteogenic differentiation and inhibit adipocyte formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Histological analysis of biopsy specimens demonstrates the presence of well-formed mineralized bone tissue with an associated bone marrow component, while cartilage is absent, indicating that ectopic bone formation occurs predominantly through intramembranous ossification rather than endochondral ossification.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical History</bold>
</p>
        <p>Though PHP&#x000a0;is typically diagnosed in early childhood, it may be detected at other life stages due to its rarity and heterogeneous presentations. PHP/its spectrum should be considered even if&#x000a0;1 major criterion is present (see<bold> Table 3 </bold>and&#x000a0;<bold>Table 4</bold>), as many physical (and biochemical) findings of the PHP evolve through childhood, and there is marked variability in disease presentation and clinical severity with the same mutation, so it may not be recognizable in infancy and toddlerhood. There may or may not be an obvious family history of the disease, depending on which parent the child inherited the disease from, as genetic imprinting can mask its expression.</p>
        <p>The presence of the AHO&#x000a0;phenotype should prompt&#x000a0;clinicians to complete a biochemical workup. The term AHO denotes a clustering of physical features, ie, a round face, a stocky habitus with short stature, brachydactyly, and ectopic ossification. Short bones are not present at birth and result from premature epiphyseal closure, leading to reduced growth. Short bones are more marked acrally (the hands and feet). Later developmental delay, early-onset obesity, and relative macrocephaly were described. Advanced bone age in a short child and ectopic ossification are other things to keep in mind when we suspect PHP. Early-onset obesity may be the only presenting symptom in many children, and other features may evolve. Children with acrodysostosis may have been born small for gestational age.</p>
        <p>Patients with hypocalcemia may report paresthesias, perioral numbness, and muscle cramping or spasms. Evaluation should include assessment for additional hormone resistance involving TSH, LH, FSH, and GHRH, which may manifest as delayed puberty and short stature. Growth hormone deficiency, beyond its effects on bone growth as previously described, also contributes to reduced linear growth.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>A physical exam for PHP 1b would likely only find symptomatic hypocalcemia. Hypocalcemia can present with positive Chvostek and Trousseau signs. The Chvostek sign is positive when tapping the facial nerve elicits contraction of the facial muscles on the same side, due to the nerve's hyperexcitability. A positive Trousseau sign occurs when inflating a sphygmomanometer to a pressure above systolic blood pressure and maintaining that pressure for 3 minutes leads to transient ischemia and carpal spasm due to hypocalcemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;In rare cases, the patient might present with seizures, and an imaging of the brain may be needed to rule out any cerebral pathology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Ectopic ossification requires evaluation and differentiation from calcification. This process reflects differentiation of adipose-derived progenitor mesenchymal stem cells into bone and represents a hallmark of <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> gene loss of function. Nearly all patients with POH and PPHP/AHO demonstrate ectopic ossification, with variable frequency also observed in PHP 1a and PHP 1b.</p>
        <p>Brachydactyly type E refers to shortening of the metacarpals with usually normal phalangeal length, with or without shortened metatarsals. The fifth, fourth, and third metacarpals and the first and fourth distal phalanges represent the most commonly affected bones of the hand. Turner syndrome, tricho-rhino-phalangeal syndrome, and brachydactyly&#x02013;intellectual disability syndrome represent additional conditions associated with brachydactyly.</p>
        <p>A recent case report identified hypokalemia as the presenting sign of pseudohypoparathyroidism.&#x000a0;PHP 1a and 1c may present with hypocalcemia while also demonstrating the&#x000a0;AHO phenotype.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27940.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
