                <td style="height: 27px; width: 523.312px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Short stature, round face, brachydactyly, subcutaneous ossifications, and developmental delay</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 14px;">
                <td style="height: 14px; width: 281.688px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Supportive features</td>
                <td style="height: 14px; width: 523.312px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Endocrine:&#x000a0;</bold>Elevated TSH/unexplained congenital hypothyroidism, hypogonadism, hypercalcitoninaemia, GH deficiency</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Neurological/CNS-skeletal</bold>:&#x000a0;Cognitive impairment, hearing impairment, spinal stenosis, Chiari malformation type 1, syringomyelia, carpal tunnel syndrome, craniosynostosis</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Mineralization/dental/eye</bold>:&#x000a0;Enamel hypoplasia, delayed tooth eruption or tooth ankylosis, oligodontia/hypodontia, advanced skeletal maturation, cataract, CNS calcifications</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Other multisystem features</bold>:&#x000a0;Sleep apnoea, recurrent ear infections, asthma/wheezing, early-onset obesity, small-for-gestational-age, cryptorchidism</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>PTH resistance has a wide age range of onset, and hypocalcemia is the last event, usually preceded by PTH elevation and followed by phosphorus elevation. The lag between PTH elevation and hypocalcemia has been observed to be approximately 4.5 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r15">[15]</xref> Serum calcium, phosphorus, and parathyroid hormone levels should be checked together, as should 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels.&#x000a0;The presence of hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D, and elevated parathyroid hormone levels suggests PHP. Renal insufficiency and hypomagnesemia should be ruled out when labeling a patient with PHP for the first time. A synthetic PTH challenge test (Ellsworth-Howard test) can be performed, but is not necessary for diagnosis.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>PHP1 patients should have biochemical testing performed annually, including PTH, calcium, phosphate, TSH, and urinary calcium-to-creatinine ratio.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Monitoring appropriate height and growth is important, and evaluation for growth hormone deficiency should be performed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Screening (and treatment) for other endocrinopathies, eg, hypogonadism and hypercalcitoninemia, should be individualized.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;X-rays of the upper and lower extremities are recommended during the initial evaluation to screen for brachydactyly or other bone malformations (see <bold>Image</bold>. Pseudohypoparathyroidism).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>The presence of 1 or more major criteria (see <bold>Table 3</bold>) should prompt molecular genetic analysis, which remains essential for establishing the diagnosis, particularly in the setting of overlapping clinical features, challenges in subtype classification, and implications for genetic counseling. Molecular testing strategies must accurately detect point mutations, genetic or genomic rearrangements, and methylation defects.</p>
        <p>In patients presenting with the AHO phenotype, sequencing of the <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> region or related genes should serve as the initial approach. In contrast, patients with PTH resistance without the AHO phenotype should undergo copy-number and methylation analyses of the <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> region as the first step. After identification of a pathogenic variant, determination of the parent of origin is necessary. In cases of strong clinical suspicion with negative results and no family history, consideration of parental germline mosaicism remains appropriate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r10">[10]</xref> Point mutations (42%) and imprinting defects affecting all 4 <italic toggle="yes">GNAS</italic> differentially methylated regions (41%) represented the most frequent variants in a large European cohort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r21">[21]</xref> A simplified diagnostic algorithm may guide molecular evaluation of pseudohypoparathyroidism and related disorders (see <bold>Image</bold>. Confirmation of Pseudohypoparathyroidism).</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>An interprofessional follow-up and early, specific interventions are key in the treatment of patients with PHP and related disorders.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Parathyroid Resistance</bold>
</p>
        <p>Treatment with active vitamin D with or without calcium supplementation is indicated in the presence of progressive PTH elevation with hyperphosphatemia or PTH levels greater than 2 times the upper limit of normal, even in the absence of hypocalcemia. Therapeutic goals include maintaining serum calcium and phosphorus within normal ranges, achieving PTH levels in the upper normal range, and avoiding hypercalciuria. Maintaining PTH in the upper normal range supports distal tubular calcium reabsorption and reduces urinary calcium loss, whereas excessive PTH may adversely affect bone and the growth plate.</p>
        <p>Calcitriol or alphacalcidiol, with or without calcium supplementation, generally achieves these targets. Dose adjustments should maintain serum calcium at the lower end of the normal range. Monitoring should include serum calcium and 24-hour urinary calcium to prevent nephrolithiasis and renal dysfunction. Achieving all biochemical targets may not always be feasible; however, a close approximation remains clinically appropriate. Preserved distal tubular responsiveness to PTH reduces the risk of nephrolithiasis compared with hypoparathyroidism and permits relatively more aggressive correction of hypocalcemia.</p>
        <p>Monitoring of PTH, calcium, and phosphorus should occur at least every 6 months in children and annually in adults, with more frequent assessment during rapid growth, dose adjustments, pregnancy, and breastfeeding.&#x000a0;Brain imaging to assess calcifications is recommended only in symptomatic individuals. Renal imaging should be performed in cases of persistent hypercalciuria. Dental evaluations every 6 to 12 months are recommended in growing children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Acute management requires intravenous calcium in symptomatic patients, including those with spasms or seizures, a prolonged QT interval on electrocardiogram, a corrected calcium level less than 7.5 mg/dL, or high-risk features. Initial treatment includes 1 to 2 g of calcium gluconate administered over 10 to 20 minutes, followed by continuous infusion. Infusion may be prepared by diluting 1000 mg elemental calcium in 1 L of 5% dextrose or normal saline and administering at approximately 1 to 2 cc/kg/hr. Transition to oral therapy should occur as early as possible. Oral calcium carbonate, 2 to 8 g elemental calcium daily, and calcitriol, 0.25 to 1.0 &#x000b5;g twice daily, generally achieve adequate control and allow discontinuation of intravenous therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Treatment with parathyroid hormone or parathyroid hormone analogs is not recommended.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Thyroid Hormone Resistance</bold>
</p>
        <p>TSH should be tested at diagnosis and thereafter every 6 months in patients younger than 5 years and yearly in older individuals. Treatment thresholds, formulations for treatment, and monitoring should be performed as for any other cause of primary hypothyroidism.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Growth</bold>
</p>
        <p>Growth should be monitored every 6 months, with growth hormone stimulation testing performed when growth deceleration occurs. Recombinant human growth hormone (rhGH) is recommended for children with confirmed growth hormone deficiency. Children born small for gestational age who fail to demonstrate catch-up growth may also receive rhGH therapy, while decisions regarding rhGH use in children without growth hormone deficiency should be individualized based on clinical context.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Puberty and Fertility</bold>
</p>
        <p>Biochemical testing and treatment for other forms of hypogonadism should be followed in affected individuals with PHP and related disorders. Treatment of hypogonadism and infertility should also follow current regional and international recommendations. However, the chances of vaginal birth may be limited due to the small pelvis in these women.&#x000a0;In addition to these clinically significant hormone resistances, calcitonin resistance with hypercalcitoninemia and occasional prolactinemia have been observed in these individuals; however, the clinical significance of these findings remains unknown, and routine screening is not recommended.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Obesity and Metabolic Consequences</bold>
</p>
        <p>Obesity and metabolic consequences may present as early as infancy, primarily driven by reduced energy expenditure rather than hyperphagia, with a tendency to diminish with age. Increased prevalence of obstructive sleep apnea, impaired glucose metabolism, lipid abnormalities, and hypertension warrants routine screening and management according to current standards of care. Preventive counseling should address weight management and strategies to reduce long-term metabolic risk.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ectopic Ossification</bold>
</p>
        <p>Ectopic ossification represents a frequent manifestation of PHP and related disorders, particularly in paternally inherited subtypes. Each clinical visit should include assessment for superficial cutaneous bony plaques, with attention to the location and size of ossifications, joint involvement, and associated limitations in movement. Evaluation should also include assessment of pressure areas, eg, the feet and ankles, and identification of potential triggering factors, including trauma, infection, or inflammation. Imaging is indicated when surgical intervention is under consideration due to pain, restricted mobility, or involvement of critical structures. Initial management includes physical therapy and meticulous skin care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Brachydactyly and Orthopedic Issues</bold>
</p>
        <p>Brachydactyly and orthopedic issues should be evaluated and managed on an individual basis, depending on the degree of functional impairment. Cognitive function impairment should be assessed at the time of diagnosis, and support should be provided as required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>A correct diagnosis is very important for this condition, which has a variable presentation and evolving manifestations, and shares many overlapping features with common conditions, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypocalcemia with elevated PTH:&#x000a0;</bold>vitamin D deficiency or resistant rickets</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia (but low PTH)</bold>: hypoparathyroidism (but PTH may be spuriously low due to its strict pre-analytical needs, and should be kept in mind)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Brachydactyly</bold>:&#x000a0;Differentials include hypoparathyroidism due to PTH gene mutations, tricho-rhino-phalangeal syndrome (<italic toggle="yes">TRPS1</italic> mutations), isolated brachydactyly type E (<italic toggle="yes">HOXD13</italic> mutations), brachydactyly-mental retardation syndrome (2q37 microdeletions), Turner syndrome, and brachydactyly type E with short stature (<italic toggle="yes">PTHLH</italic> mutations)<bold>Ossifications (subcutaneous)</bold>: Cutaneous tumors, secondary/traumatic osteoma cutis, miliary osteoma, fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva (progressive ossification, clue post trauma/immunization), tumoral calcinosis (<italic toggle="yes">FGF23/GALNT3</italic> mutations).<bold>Early-onset obesity:&#x000a0;</bold>Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, genetic/cytogenetic/syndromic obesity (Prader-Willi, POMC, MC4R, leptin/leptin receptor mutations).<bold>Early-onset hypothyroidism</bold>: Includes congenital hypothyroidism (any cause) and TSH resistance (TSH receptor mutations)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Magnesium deficiency and renal insufficiency should be ruled out before labeling an individual with PHP.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Due to the rarity of these conditions, information regarding long -term prognosis is limited. These conditions involve variable organ dysfunction and limitations depending on the subtype, so the prognosis of PHP remains variable. The condition itself (hypocalcemia, obesity, ectopic ossifications, intellectual disability, hypogonadism) or its associated comorbidities (nephrocalcinosis, sleep apnea, cardiometabolic complications) increase morbidity and possibly mortality compared to the general population. Nevertheless, milder presentations may have a near-normal life with adequate support and treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r23">[23]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The following complications are associated with&#x000a0;PHP and related conditions:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Complications related to PTH resistance: chronic hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia, and their treatment have risks of tetany, nephrocalcinosis, calcifications of parts of the basal ganglia (Fahr syndrome), cataract, epilepsy, and possibly&#x000a0;Parkinsonism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r24">[24]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Complications related to musculoskeletal features of PHP and related conditions: Children with brachydactyly may suffer from difficulty with fine motor skills,&#x000a0;an increased prevalence of carpal tunnel syndrome seen in AHO patients, and these conditions may be associated with higher than normal, as well as an earlier presentation of spinal stenosis, which can lead to lower extremity paraparesis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r27">[27]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Obesity and its complications: More than a 4-fold increased risk for sleep apnea is reported in childhood. Obesity-related complications may contribute to reduced life expectancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r28">[28]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cognitive disability: A significant number of children with these conditions have cognitive dysfunction and need support to help them do age-appropriate tasks.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Other endocrinopathies: an increased burden of treatment and complications due to hypothyroidism, hypogonadism and infertility, and growth hormone deficiency are seen in these individuals.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Complications related to ectopic ossifications: Ectopic ossifications pose huge challenges in some of these individuals, requiring physical therapy, surgeries, or even amputation sometimes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27940.r23">[23]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>The following team members may be involved in the management of PHP and related disorders:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatric endocrinologists play a pivotal role due to the involvement of multiple endocrine organs, short stature, and obesity affecting a large chunk of individuals.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Medical geneticist: for diagnosis and possible future risks in progeny.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Developmental pediatrician, occupational and physical therapist: for children with intellectual disability and acrodysostosis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Physical therapist: for individuals with ectopic ossifications.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dietitian and physical trainer: for overweight and obese individuals, ENT/pulmonology/sleep specialists' opinion for sleep apnea.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fertility specialists: in adulthood</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurology, radiology, and psychiatry referrals for individuals with relevant complications.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>As these are very rare conditions, awareness among physicians and other health care&#x000a0;practitioners may be limited; therefore, awareness should be improved, and a high index of suspicion maintained to facilitate diagnosis. PHP is an inherited disorder, with a complex genetic mechanism underlying different subtypes, and imprinting adds to these complexities. Genetic testing and counseling are recommended for individuals with PHP, especially at a childbearing age, so they can fully identify their risk of passing on the disease. The help of a qualified geneticist with experience in these disorders should be sought.&#x000a0;For affected individuals, interprofessional team management is necessary to screen for comorbidities and complications and to provide timely interventions for involved organ systems.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>PHP is a rare group of inactivating PTH/PTHrP signaling disorders (iPPSD) caused by genetic or epigenetic alterations of the GNAS locus, leading to end-organ resistance to parathyroid hormone with hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and elevated PTH levels. Patients may present with Albright hereditary osteodystrophy features, ectopic ossification, brachydactyly, growth impairment, obesity, and variable multihormone resistance. Diagnosis requires an integrated biochemical evaluation of calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D, supported by targeted molecular and epigenetic testing, and is informed by evolving classification systems that improve diagnostic precision. Management focuses on maintaining calcium-phosphate balance with active vitamin D and calcium supplementation, monitoring renal and endocrine complications, and addressing associated growth, skeletal, and metabolic abnormalities.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration is essential to optimize outcomes and safety through coordinated, patient-centered care. Physicians and endocrinologists lead diagnosis, treatment planning, and longitudinal endocrine management. Primary care clinicians support early recognition, routine monitoring, and preventive care coordination. Genetic specialists guide molecular confirmation and recurrence counseling. Nurses ensure ongoing monitoring, symptom surveillance, and patient education. Pharmacists optimize the use of calcium, calcitriol, and related therapies while monitoring safety and adherence. Allied health professionals, including dietitians, physical and occupational therapists, and sleep and fertility specialists, address obesity, functional impairment, and comorbid conditions. Integrated communication and shared decision-making enable timely referrals, complication prevention, and individualized long-term care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27940.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
